        author: "Takehiko Inoue", 
        genre: "Historical Fiction", 
        volumes: "Hiatus",
        image: "images/vagabond.jpg",
        synopsis: "A fictionalized, highly philosophical retelling tracking the brutal combat evolution and spiritual maturity of Japan's legendary sword saint, Miyamoto Musashi.",
        chapterTitle: "Chapter 1: Shinmen Takezo",
        storyContent: "SCENE 1: THE BLOOD MORASS\nIn the year 1600, the historic Battle of Sekigahara has just ended in a catastrophic defeat. 17-year-old Shinmen Takezo (the boy who will eventually become the legendary sword saint Miyamoto Musashi) blinks awake, face down in a freezing swamp of mud, rain, and thousands of decaying soldier corpses.\n\nSCENE 2: THE SCAVENGER ATTACK\nAs Takezo pulls himself up, a squad of brutal Tokugawa scavenger soldiers marches through the dense fog, stabbing wounded survivors with long iron spears to harvest armor. Takezo stays dead silent, his fingers tightening around a nicked, mud-stained katana lying near his hand.\n\nSCENE 3: THE WILD BEAST LAUNCH\nWhen a soldier steps near, Takezo lunges from the mud like a feral, predatory beast. Moving with raw, unpolished, and terrifying speed, he slashes through the first two guards, his eyes burning with an intense survival instinct that terrifies the remaining scouts, who flee into the brush.\n\nSCENE 4: THE WILL TO LIVE\nTakezo drags his childhood friend Matahachi, who is heavily wounded, out of the swamp and into the safety of a deep mountain cave. As he collapses against the stone wall clutching his own bleeding side, Takezo stares out at the stormy sky, letting out a ragged roar: 'I am not dying in this mud. I am going to become invincible under the sun.'"
